  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nThe upper level ridge axis extends across our region, with SW \nbeginning to build over the western Dakotas ahead of a deepening \nupper level trough in the Northern Rockies/Pacific NW. This trough \npassage and the associated front will bring the next round of \norganized showers and thunderstorms (and severe risk) to our region \nMonday, but may also draw in additional smoke aloft and potentially \na brief period of surface level smoke behind the cold front. This \ntrough becomes a more dominate feature for our region through the \nweek as it stalls and deepens in Canada, and flattens the larger \nscale ridging. This will support temperatures in seasonal ranges for \nmost days next week (+/1 1 standard deviation of normal), and a \nbreak from the much hotter temperatures in the 90+ range through \nnext weekend. The tendency for at most very weak mid level waves in \nmid/upper level westerly or weak northwest flow lowers predictability \nfor any periods when measurable precipitation may occur and any \nthunderstorm impacts after Monday. \n\n...Severe Weather Risk Tonight/Monday Morning...\n\nInitially strong capping in the 850-700MB layer and the lack of \norganized forcing should limit the potential for thunderstorm \ninitiation through this evening. Better signal for thunderstorm \narrives as forcing increases as the cold front moves into the region \nwhile a LLJ/elevated WAA remain in place Monday morning (generally \n6am-10am). Elevated instability and increasing effective shear could \nsupport a large hail/damaging wind threat with thunderstorms that \ndevelop Monday morning.  \n\n...Severe Weather Risk Monday Afternoon/Evening...\n\nCAMs continue to show ongoing elevated shower and thunderstorms \nthrough Monday afternoon as the main trough moves across the region. \nIf smoke aloft is too thick or mid level clouds/elevated storms \nlinger, severe threats may remain isolated/marginal in nature with \nstronger capping in place. However where there is more clearing, \nCAMs are showing a signal for EML development ahead of the front \nwith strongly sheared turning hodographs supportive of supercells. \nThere should be enough forcing with the trough and front in that \nscenario for the CAP to be eroded near the frontal zone, and a more \norganized severe threat in the afternoon/evening. CAMs do show the \nstronger UH tracks/supercell type signal with some showing bowing \ntype MCS clusters and this reflects the higher level scenario that \nis conditional on better surface based destabilization.",
